Coordinates
coordFlip() turns the plot sideways while the mappings stay as written. This is the usual way to give long category labels room.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'manufacturer', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;boxplot(fill: '#C6DBEF')
    -&gt;coordFlip()
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Highway mpg');

coordCartesian(xlim:, ylim:), xlim(), and ylim() fix the domains and clip marks outside them. ggplot2’s xlim() removes the data outside the limits, which changes statistics such as smoothers. phiz keeps the data and only zooms.
coordFixed(ratio) fixes the aspect ratio, as coord_fixed() does: one unit of y takes ratio times the length of one unit of x. The panel’s height follows from its width (300 pixels unless set) and the data ranges, and the scales keep those ranges exactly. coord_equal() is the same with a ratio of 1.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'cty', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.5)
    -&gt;abline(color: 'gray', linetype: 'dashed')
    -&gt;coordFixed()
    -&gt;labs(x: 'City mpg', y: 'Highway mpg');

coordPolar() draws bar() and col() layers in polar coordinates. theta: 'y' stacks the bars into a pie (leave x unmapped, or map it to a constant), and innerRadius: in pixels makes a donut. theta: 'x' makes a rose: one equal slice per level of x, with radius y. start: rotates the first slice clockwise from twelve o’clock, in radians, and direction: -1 runs anticlockwise. Other geoms have no polar form in Vega-Lite, and phiz rejects them.
use Phiz\Compose;
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$cars = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg());

$plot = Compose::beside(
    $cars-&gt;aes(fill: 'class')-&gt;bar()-&gt;coordPolar(theta: 'y')-&gt;title('Pie'),
    $cars-&gt;aes(x: 'class', fill: 'class')-&gt;bar()-&gt;coordPolar()-&gt;title('Rose'),
)-&gt;resolve(['scale' =&gt; ['theta' =&gt; 'independent', 'radius' =&gt; 'independent']]);

Livewire
The component re-renders inside Livewire without extra code. The specification is an attribute of the container, which Livewire’s morphing updates on each render. The drawn chart sits in a child element marked wire:ignore, and the phiz loader embeds the new specification when the attribute changes. When a component renders several charts, give each a wire:key.
class RentChart extends Component
{
    public int $bedrooms = 2;

    public function render()
    {
        $plot = Plot::of(Rental::where('bedrooms', $this-&gt;bedrooms)-&gt;get())
            -&gt;aes(x: 'area_m2', y: 'rent', color: 'neighborhood')
            -&gt;point(alpha: 0.6)
            -&gt;smooth(method: 'lm', se: false);

        return view('livewire.rent-chart', ['plot' =&gt; $plot]);
    }
}
&lt;div&gt;
    &lt;select wire:model.live="bedrooms"&gt;...&lt;/select&gt;
    &lt;x-phiz :plot="$plot" wire:key="rents" :options="['theme' =&gt; 'auto']" /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
'theme' =&gt; 'auto' follows the reader’s light or dark mode preference. Dashboards with Livewire builds a complete example with several linked charts.

Install phiz in a project with composer require phpstats/phiz, then save this script as chart.php beside the vendor directory:
&lt;?php

require __DIR__.'/vendor/autoload.php';

use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'drv')
    -&gt;point()
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg', color: 'Drive');

$plot-&gt;save('chart.html');
php chart.php writes chart.html, a standalone page. Open it in a browser to see the chart. The page loads vega, vega-lite, and vega-embed from the jsDelivr CDN, so viewing it needs a network connection. save('chart.svg'), save('chart.png'), and save('chart.pdf') write static files instead and need the vl-convert binary (Rendering).

