Data, mappings, and geoms
A plot starts with data and aesthetic mappings. Plot::of() takes the data, aes() maps fields to visual channels, and a geom such as point() adds a layer. The bundled Datasets class provides a few classic datasets for examples.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point();

Mapping another field to color gives each class its own color and adds a legend. Fixed properties, such as a point size or an opacity, are geom arguments.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'class')
    -&gt;point(size: 40, alpha: 0.7);

Layers
Each geom adds a layer, and layers inherit the plot’s mappings. This plot overlays a loess smoother on the points and labels the axes.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.4)
    -&gt;smooth(span: 0.6)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg');

Plots are immutable. Every method returns a new plot, so a base plot can be stored and extended in several directions.
$base = Plot::of($mpg)-&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy');

$scatter = $base-&gt;point();
$smooth = $base-&gt;point(alpha: 0.3)-&gt;smooth(method: 'lm');

Pipes
PHP 8.5’s pipe operator composes ggplot2-style functions. ggplot() starts a plot, and each geom_*(), scale_*(), labs(), or theme_*() function returns a closure that takes a plot and returns the next one. The method chain builds the same specification on every supported PHP version.

PHP 8.5+PHP 8.3–8.4


use Phiz\Datasets;

use function Phiz\{aes, geom_point, geom_smooth, ggplot, labs, theme_minimal};

$plot = ggplot(Datasets::mpg(), aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'drv')) |&gt;
    geom_point(alpha: 0.5) |&gt;
    geom_smooth(method: 'lm', se: false) |&gt;
    labs(x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg', color: 'Drive') |&gt;
    theme_minimal();


use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

use function Phiz\aes;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg(), aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'drv'))
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.5)
    -&gt;smooth(method: 'lm', se: false)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg', color: 'Drive')
    -&gt;theme('minimal');




The functions and the methods accept the same arguments. On PHP 8.3 and 8.4, apply() also takes the functions: ggplot($mpg, aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy'))-&gt;apply(geom_point(), labs(x: 'Displacement (L)')). A plot can also apply reusable fragments with apply():
$house = fn (Plot $plot) =&gt; $plot-&gt;theme('minimal', baseSize: 12)-&gt;size(480, 300);

$plot = Plot::of($mpg)-&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy')-&gt;point()-&gt;apply($house);

Statistics

Coefficient plot
Straight from a phit fit (fit_lm, fit_glm, …).

coefplot($fit)
    -&gt;labs(title: 'mpg ~ wt + hp + am');


Comparing models
Several fits, dodged.

coefplot(['Full' =&gt; $fit, 'Without am' =&gt; $reduced]);


Regression diagnostics
Residuals, QQ, scale-location, observed vs fitted.

diagnostic_plots($fit, width: 230, height: 170);


Predicted values
Predictions with 95% bands as weight varies, by transmission.

effect_plot($fit, $mtcars, 'wt', by: 'am', design: fn ($r) =&gt; [1, $r['wt'], $r['hp'], $r['am']])
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Weight (1000 lbs)', y: 'mpg', color: 'Manual', fill: 'Manual');


Posterior intervals
From CmdStanPHP draws_columns(), as bayesplot’s mcmc_intervals.

interval_plot($draws);


Means with error bars
Precomputed summaries with errorbar and point.

Plot::of(array_map(function ($class) use ($mpg) {
        $values = array_column(array_filter($mpg, fn ($r) =&gt; $r['class'] === $class), 'hwy');
        $mean = array_sum($values) / count($values);
        $sd = sqrt(array_sum(array_map(fn ($v) =&gt; ($v - $mean) ** 2, $values)) / (count($values) - 1));
        $se = $sd / sqrt(count($values));

        return ['class' =&gt; $class, 'mean' =&gt; $mean, 'lower' =&gt; $mean - 1.96 * $se, 'upper' =&gt; $mean + 1.96 * $se];
    }, array_values(array_unique(array_column($mpg, 'class')))))
        -&gt;aes(x: 'class', y: 'mean', ymin: 'lower', ymax: 'upper')
        -&gt;errorbar(width: 8)
        -&gt;point(size: 50)
        -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Mean highway mpg (95% CI)');


Summaries
stat_summary: means with t intervals over the raw data.

Plot::of($mpg)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'class', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.2)
    -&gt;summary(funData: 'mean_cl_normal', color: '#D62728')
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Highway mpg');


Functions
curve() draws a PHP function, here the normal density over a histogram.

Plot::of(array_map(fn ($i) =&gt; ['z' =&gt; Fit::normalQuantile(($i + 0.5) / 500)], range(0, 499)))
    -&gt;aes(x: 'z')
    -&gt;histogram(binwidth: 0.25, density: true, fill: '#DDDDDD')
    -&gt;curve(fn (float $x) =&gt; exp(-$x * $x / 2) / sqrt(2 * M_PI), -3.5, 3.5, aes: aes(x: 'z', y: 'density'), color: '#D62728', linewidth: 2);

Aggregates and time units
Shorthand also covers Vega-Lite’s aggregates and time units: 'mean(hwy)', 'count()', 'median(price)', 'year(date):O', and 'yearmonth(date)'. The aggregation runs in Vega-Lite, grouped by the other mapped fields.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::economics())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'year(date):O', y: 'mean(psavert)')
    -&gt;col(fill: '#4C78A8')
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Mean personal savings rate (%)');

Computed fields
calc() maps an aesthetic to a field computed from a Vega expression. The expression refers to the row as datum.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

use function Phiz\calc;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'cty', y: calc('datum.hwy - datum.cty', as: 'gap'))
    -&gt;jitter(width: 0.3, height: 0.3, alpha: 0.5)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'City mpg', y: 'Highway minus city mpg');

The charts
fuel_dashboard() takes the cars table and the filter state and returns three plots and the class summary behind the bar chart. The Livewire component below calls it from render().

The bar chart shows mean highway mpg by class, from phplyr’s group_by() and summarize(). The selected class is highlighted with a computed field.
The scatterplot shows the filtered cars, with one linear fit across all of them.
The trend chart compares each manufacturer’s mean in 1999 and 2008. It ignores the year filter, so the comparison stays visible.


PHP 8.5+PHP 8.3–8.4


/*
 * The fuel economy dashboard from the "Dashboards with Livewire" documentation page: three charts built from phplyr
 * summaries of one filtered phibble table. A Livewire component calls fuel_dashboard() in render() with its public
 * properties, so every chart follows the same filters.
 */

use Phibble\Contracts\Table;
use Phiz\Plot;

use function Phiz\aes;

use function Phplyr\{arrange, desc, filter, group_by, mean, n, summarize};

/**
 * @param string|null $drive 'f', 'r', '4', or null for all
 * @param int|null $year 1999, 2008, or null for both
 * @return array{summary: Table, bars: Plot, scatter: Plot, trend: Plot}
 */
function fuel_dashboard(Table $cars, ?string $drive = null, ?int $year = null, ?string $selected = null): array
{
    $byDrive = $cars |&gt; filter(fn ($r) =&gt; $drive === null || $r-&gt;drv === $drive);
    $subset = $byDrive |&gt; filter(fn ($r) =&gt; $year === null || $r-&gt;year === $year);

    $byClass = $subset |&gt;
        group_by('class') |&gt;
        summarize(cars: n(), hwy: mean('hwy'), cty: mean('cty')) |&gt;
        arrange(desc('hwy'));

    // The trend compares the two years, so it ignores the year filter.
    $byManufacturer = $byDrive |&gt;
        group_by('manufacturer', 'year') |&gt;
        summarize(hwy: mean('hwy'));

    $bars = Plot::of($byClass)
        -&gt;aes(x: 'hwy', y: \Phiz\field('class', sort: '-x'), fill: \Phiz\calc('datum.class === '.json_encode($selected ?? ''), 'selected', 'nominal'))
        -&gt;col()
        -&gt;scaleFill(domain: [false, true], range: ['#9ECAE1', '#08519C'], guide: false)
        -&gt;tooltip(['class', 'cars', 'hwy', 'cty'])
        -&gt;labs(title: 'Mean highway mpg by class', x: 'Highway mpg', y: null)
        -&gt;size(320, 220);

    $scatter = Plot::of($subset)
        -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'class')
        -&gt;point(alpha: 0.7, size: 40)
        -&gt;smooth(aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy'), method: 'lm', color: '#333333', inheritAes: false)
        -&gt;tooltip(['manufacturer', 'model', 'year', 'displ', 'hwy'])
        -&gt;theme(legendPosition: 'bottom')
        -&gt;labs(title: 'Displacement and highway mpg', x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg', color: null)
        -&gt;size(320, 220);

    $trend = Plot::of($byManufacturer)
        -&gt;aes(x: 'year:O', y: 'hwy', group: 'manufacturer')
        -&gt;line(color: '#BBBBBB')
        -&gt;point(color: '#666666', size: 20)
        -&gt;tooltip(['manufacturer', 'year', 'hwy'])
        -&gt;labs(title: 'Manufacturers, 1999 to 2008', x: null, y: 'Mean highway mpg')
        -&gt;size(200, 220);

    return ['summary' =&gt; $byClass, 'bars' =&gt; $bars, 'scatter' =&gt; $scatter, 'trend' =&gt; $trend];
}


/*
 * The fuel economy dashboard from the "Dashboards with Livewire" documentation page, written with method chains for
 * PHP 8.3 and 8.4. examples/dashboard.php is the same code with the pipe operator; the tests check that both build the
 * same charts.
 */

use Phibble\Contracts\Table;
use Phiz\Plot;

use function Phiz\aes;

use function Phplyr\{desc, mean, n, phplyr};

/**
 * @param string|null $drive 'f', 'r', '4', or null for all
 * @param int|null $year 1999, 2008, or null for both
 * @return array{summary: Table, bars: Plot, scatter: Plot, trend: Plot}
 */
function fuel_dashboard(Table $cars, ?string $drive = null, ?int $year = null, ?string $selected = null): array
{
    $byDrive = phplyr($cars)-&gt;filter(fn ($r) =&gt; $drive === null || $r-&gt;drv === $drive)-&gt;get();
    $subset = phplyr($byDrive)-&gt;filter(fn ($r) =&gt; $year === null || $r-&gt;year === $year)-&gt;get();

    $byClass = phplyr($subset)
        -&gt;groupBy('class')
        -&gt;summarize(cars: n(), hwy: mean('hwy'), cty: mean('cty'))
        -&gt;arrange(desc('hwy'))
        -&gt;get();

    // The trend compares the two years, so it ignores the year filter.
    $byManufacturer = phplyr($byDrive)
        -&gt;groupBy('manufacturer', 'year')
        -&gt;summarize(hwy: mean('hwy'))
        -&gt;get();

    $bars = Plot::of($byClass)
        -&gt;aes(x: 'hwy', y: \Phiz\field('class', sort: '-x'), fill: \Phiz\calc('datum.class === '.json_encode($selected ?? ''), 'selected', 'nominal'))
        -&gt;col()
        -&gt;scaleFill(domain: [false, true], range: ['#9ECAE1', '#08519C'], guide: false)
        -&gt;tooltip(['class', 'cars', 'hwy', 'cty'])
        -&gt;labs(title: 'Mean highway mpg by class', x: 'Highway mpg', y: null)
        -&gt;size(320, 220);

    $scatter = Plot::of($subset)
        -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'class')
        -&gt;point(alpha: 0.7, size: 40)
        -&gt;smooth(aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy'), method: 'lm', color: '#333333', inheritAes: false)
        -&gt;tooltip(['manufacturer', 'model', 'year', 'displ', 'hwy'])
        -&gt;theme(legendPosition: 'bottom')
        -&gt;labs(title: 'Displacement and highway mpg', x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg', color: null)
        -&gt;size(320, 220);

    $trend = Plot::of($byManufacturer)
        -&gt;aes(x: 'year:O', y: 'hwy', group: 'manufacturer')
        -&gt;line(color: '#BBBBBB')
        -&gt;point(color: '#666666', size: 20)
        -&gt;tooltip(['manufacturer', 'year', 'hwy'])
        -&gt;labs(title: 'Manufacturers, 1999 to 2008', x: null, y: 'Mean highway mpg')
        -&gt;size(200, 220);

    return ['summary' =&gt; $byClass, 'bars' =&gt; $bars, 'scatter' =&gt; $scatter, 'trend' =&gt; $trend];
}



With the drive filter on front-wheel drive, the year on 2008, and the compact class selected, the charts look like this:
use Phibble\DataFrame;
use Phiz\Compose;
use Phiz\Datasets;

require_once __DIR__.'/../../examples/dashboard.php';

// The dashboard with drive = 'f', year = 2008, and the compact class selected.
$charts = fuel_dashboard(DataFrame::fromRows(Datasets::mpg()), drive: 'f', year: 2008, selected: 'compact');

$plot = Compose::beside($charts['bars'], $charts['scatter'], $charts['trend']);

The Livewire component
The component keeps the filters as public properties. render() passes them to fuel_dashboard(). Livewire calls render() after every update, so a new selection produces new specifications, and the phiz loader re-embeds each chart whose specification changed.
namespace App\Livewire;

use Livewire\Attributes\Url;
use Livewire\Component;
use Phibble\DataFrame;
use Phiz\Datasets;

class FuelDashboard extends Component
{
    #[Url]
    public ?string $drive = null;

    #[Url]
    public ?int $year = null;

    public ?string $selected = null;

    public function select(string $class): void
    {
        $this-&gt;selected = $this-&gt;selected === $class ? null : $class;
    }

    public function render()
    {
        // In an application this would be DataFrame::fromQuery(Car::query()) or a cached table.
        $cars = DataFrame::fromRows(Datasets::mpg());

        return view('livewire.fuel-dashboard', fuel_dashboard($cars, $this-&gt;drive, $this-&gt;year, $this-&gt;selected));
    }
}
The view puts the filters and the three charts side by side. Each &lt;x-phiz&gt; has a wire:key, so Livewire matches each chart to its element across renders.
